    "lawCode": "Add §236-b, County L",
    "lawSection": "County Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5713\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the county law, in relation to providing for the\nlicensure of electrical inspectors within the county of Westchester\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL :\n\nThis legislation authorizes Westchester County to establish a county\nboard of examiners for electrical inspectors. The board of examiners\nwould be empowered to assume all licensing duties within Westchester\nCounty with respect to the licensure of electrical inspectors. This\nauthorization supersedes any inconsistent general, special or local\nlaw to the contrary.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one amends the county law by adding a new section 236-b.\nSubdivision 1 of section 236-b provides that notwithstanding any\ninconsistent general, special, or local law to the contrary, the\ncounty of Westchester is authorized to establish a Westchester county\nboard of examiners for electrical inspectors and to empower such board\nto assume all licensing duties within the county of Westchester with\nrespect to the licensure of electrical inspectors. The county of\nWestchester shall provide for electrical inspectors' duties and\nresponsibilities with respect to applications for a license or renewal\nin accordance with the local law of such county. A local law enacted\npursuant to this section establishing such county licensure shall\nprovide standards for licensure which shall include, at a minimum, a\nprovision that no person shall obtain a license unless such person\nshall have received certain training which is at least equivalent to\nthe training required for code enforcement personnel under the rules\nand regulations promulgated by the governor pursuant to article six-C\nof the executive law.\n\nSubdivision 2 of section 236-b provides that a local law enacted\npursuant to t.his section establishing county licensure of electrical\ninspectors shall supersede any provision requiring electrical\ninspectors to also obtain a local license promulgated by a city, town\nor village in the county pursuant to any general, special or local\nlaw. Nothing in this section is deemed to supersede any of the powers,\nfunctions and duties of the fire fighting and code enforcement\npersonnel standards and education commission, as set forth in article\nsix-C of the executive law.\n\nSubdivision 3 of section 236-b provides a definition of the term\n\"electrical inspector.\"\n\nSubdivision 4 of section 236-b provides a definition of the term\n\"agency of the state of New York.\"\n\nSection two of the legislation provides the effective date, which is\nimmediate.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nMany local governments within Westchester County have approved and\ndesignated electrical inspectors to conduct electrical inspections in\naccordance with their enforcement obligations under the Uniform Fire\nprevention and Building Code Act. As a result of such local\nregulation, Westchester County lacks uniform countywide licensing,\nqualification and performance standards for electrical inspectors.\nEfficient oversight and monitoring designed to benefit the entire\ncounty is lacking.\nAccordingly, the licensing of electrical inspectors by Westchester\nCounty would protect the public health, safety and welfare of the\ncounty's residents by assuring that electrical inspections are\nperformed only by qualified individuals. To be qualified and thus\nobtain a county license, such individuals would have to have received\ntraining in the inspection of electrical components, equipment and\nsystems used in buildings and structures which is at least equivalent\nto the training in the inspection of electrical components, equipment\nand systems used in buildings and structures required for code\nenforcement personnel under the rules and regulations promulgated by\nthe governor pursuant to article six-C of the executive law.\n\nThe regulation of electrical inspectors at the county level would\nprovide uniformity of licensing, qualification and performance\nstandards throughout the county and remove the burden on local\nmunicipalities of monitoring the industry. Centralized oversight with\nthe potential for disciplinary proceedings that could lead to a\nlicense suspension and/or a revocation would assist in ensuring that\nelectrical inspections are performed diligently, honestly and\nprofessionally. Currently, incompetent electrical inspectors may be\nprevented from inspecting only one jurisdiction at a time. Under a\ncentralized oversight scheme, incompetent electrical inspectors may be\nprevented from inspecting on a countywide basis.\n\nWestchester County already has exclusive licensing authority over\nmaster electricians pursuant New York State County Law section 236-a.\nSince electrical inspectors are required to examine the work done by\nmaster electricians licensed by the county, it is consistent and\nsensible to also vest the county with exclusive licensing authority\nover the electrical inspectors themselves.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nA.10413/S.7322 of 2007-2008 (passed both houses)\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the State.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
